<h2 data-start=3D"1087" data-end=3D"1125" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
1090" data-end=3D"1125">1. Introduction au Lazy Loading</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"1127" data-end=3D"1462" class=3D"">Le <strong data-start=
=3D"1130" data-end=3D"1146">lazy loading</strong> est une technique qui con=
siste =C3=A0 <strong data-start=3D"1180" data-end=3D"1192">diff=C3=A9rer</s=
trong> le chargement de certaines parties d'une application jusqu'=C3=A0 ce=
 qu'elles soient r=C3=A9ellement n=C3=A9cessaires. Cela permet de r=C3=A9du=
ire le <strong data-start=3D"1323" data-end=3D"1341">bundle initial</strong=
> (taille du fichier JavaScript charg=C3=A9 au d=C3=A9marrage), ce qui am=
=C3=A9liore la rapidit=C3=A9 d'affichage et l'exp=C3=A9rience utilisateur.<=
/p>
<p data-start=3D"1464" data-end=3D"1658" class=3D"">En React, on utilise <c=
ode data-start=3D"1485" data-end=3D"1497">React.lazy</code> pour charger de=
s composants <strong data-start=3D"1526" data-end=3D"1551">de mani=C3=A8re =
asynchrone</strong>, et <code data-start=3D"1556" data-end=3D"1566">Suspens=
e</code> pour afficher une interface utilisateur temporaire (fallback) en a=
ttendant leur chargement.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"1660" data-end=3D"1663" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"1665" data-end=3D"1710" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
1668" data-end=3D"1710">2. Pourquoi utiliser le Lazy Loading ?</strong></h2=
>
<h3 data-start=3D"1712" data-end=3D"1731" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
1716" data-end=3D"1731">Avantages :</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1732" data-end=3D"2047">
<li data-start=3D"1732" data-end=3D"1841" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1734" data-end=3D"1841" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
734" data-end=3D"1778">R=C3=A9duction du temps de chargement initial</stron=
g> =E2=86=92 Seules les ressources essentielles sont charg=C3=A9es en premi=
er.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1842" data-end=3D"1961" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1844" data-end=3D"1961" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
844" data-end=3D"1877">Optimisation des performances</strong> =E2=86=92 Les=
 composants sont charg=C3=A9s =C3=A0 la demande, =C3=A9conomisant ainsi la =
m=C3=A9moire et le CPU.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1962" data-end=3D"2047" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1964" data-end=3D"2047" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
964" data-end=3D"2000">Meilleure exp=C3=A9rience utilisateur</strong> =E2=
=86=92 L'interface est interactive plus rapidement.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"2049" data-end=3D"2089" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
2053" data-end=3D"2089">Quand utiliser le Lazy Loading ?</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"2090" data-end=3D"2352">
<li data-start=3D"2090" data-end=3D"2191" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"2092" data-end=3D"2191" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"2=
092" data-end=3D"2141">Pour charger des composants rarement utilis=C3=A9s</=
strong> (ex. : modales, pages d'administration, widgets).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2192" data-end=3D"2272" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"2194" data-end=3D"2272" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"2=
194" data-end=3D"2237">Dans les applications avec React Router</strong> =E2=
=86=92 Charger les pages dynamiquement.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2273" data-end=3D"2352" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"2275" data-end=3D"2352" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"2=
275" data-end=3D"2305">Pour les composants lourds</strong> =E2=86=92 Ceux q=
ui incluent des biblioth=C3=A8ques tierces.</p>
